The previous cut excluded `this` on the grounds that its receiver type IS known
(the enclosing class), so there is no want of receiver evidence. That reasoning
does not survive contact with the emit site: an edge reaches it BECAUSE the call
did not resolve, and a this.m() that does not resolve is one whose m is NOT on
the enclosing class. The analyzer knows which object; it does not know which
type declares the method -- exactly what the two taint gates read the key for.

MEASURED HARM, CONTROL IN THE SAME RUN. this.doFinal(plain) emits
java:external:0-0:doFinal:unresolved -- bare short name, `external` in the
module slot. _build_sanitizer_index_multi indexes the shipped
javax.crypto.Cipher.doFinal under its bare last component too, so with no
call_construct the edge fell to the registrar's PERMIT branch and registered a
PHANTOM BARRIER; the same edge stamped `method` registers nothing. Since PR #214
a barrier earns `sanitized`, which DROPS the flow from the claim's violation set.

test_this_receiver_phantom_barrier.py pins both arms plus a correctly-typed
receiver that must still bind and a wrongly-typed one that must not. It is filed
in hypergumbo-core rather than beside the analyzer because a stamp test that
passes while the guard it feeds is inert is the exact shape that made WI-sajis
necessary.

PARITY, NOT A NEW CONVENTION, and the recall cost is measured rather than
assumed. python, go, objc, rust, scala and swift all stamp an explicit
this/self; java alone did not. gate_named_entry returns None immediately for
call_construct == "method", so the stamp can only refuse a FUNCTION-kind
catalogue hit -- and io_primitives/java.yaml declares 139 method-kind
primitives, 3 attribute-kind and no function-kind entry at all.

A/B on 218,049 java call edges (clover, confluent-examples, gradle):
  +85 edges gained the stamp
    0 changed dst
    0 changed resolution
Small, and stated as small: the argument is the phantom barrier and the parity,
not the volume.

AN IMPLICIT `this` -- a bare m(p) with no receiver token -- STAYS UNSTAMPED, and
that is a different omission rather than the same one half-done. call_construct
names the SYNTACTIC construct (ADR-0024, audit-findings 0012); a bare call is not
syntactically a method call, and stamping it would file a resolution fact under a
construct name. java has no free functions, so that shape IS a live
phantom-barrier surface; it needs a signal of its own and is filed separately.

One existing test asserted meta by whole-dict equality, which made it also assert
the ABSENCE of every key anyone might add later; it now asserts its own subject
(the Site-1 enclosing_class hint) key by key, with the new key asserted
explicitly. The inherited_calls linker reads enclosing_class and
inherited_field_receiver, never call_construct, so Site-1 recovery is unchanged.

